1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,410
Here's something most
visitors never find out.

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:06,690
The famous Las Vegas Strip,
the casinos, the fountains,

3
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:09,910
the giant hotels, isn't actually
in the city of Las Vegas.

4
00:00:10,845 --> 00:00:13,135
The city drinks most of its water twice.

5
00:00:13,545 --> 00:00:17,515
In the nineteen fifties, its hotels
threw parties for nuclear explosions.

6
00:00:17,825 --> 00:00:21,275
And right now, under its streets,
hundreds of people are living in the dark.

7
00:00:22,202 --> 00:00:24,562
This is a city of more
than two million people,

8
00:00:24,602 --> 00:00:27,712
in a desert that gets about
four inches of rain a year.

9
00:00:27,882 --> 00:00:29,592
By every rule, it shouldn't exist.

10
00:00:30,429 --> 00:00:32,029
So how does Las Vegas really work?

11
00:00:32,069 --> 00:00:33,259
And what keeps it alive?

12
00:00:38,657 --> 00:00:39,827
Let's start with the name.

13
00:00:39,997 --> 00:00:41,727
Las Vegas is Spanish for "the meadows."

14
00:00:42,385 --> 00:00:44,935
Because two hundred years ago,
in the middle of this desert,

15
00:00:44,985 --> 00:00:45,755
there was grass.

16
00:00:46,385 --> 00:00:49,695
Springs came up out of the ground here,
and turned a patch of the valley green.

17
00:00:50,205 --> 00:00:52,755
Travelers crossing the Mojave
stopped here for water.

18
00:00:53,675 --> 00:00:56,215
In nineteen-oh-five, a
railroad came through,

19
00:00:56,255 --> 00:00:59,765
and the railroad company decided to
sell the land around its station.

20
00:01:00,215 --> 00:01:02,155
On the fifteenth of
May, it held an auction,

21
00:01:02,195 --> 00:01:04,485
in heat of about a
hundred and ten degrees.

22
00:01:04,895 --> 00:01:07,265
That auction is considered
the birthday of the city.

23
00:01:07,685 --> 00:01:10,695
The new town drilled wells,
and many of them were artesian:

24
00:01:10,845 --> 00:01:12,995
the water shot out of
the ground by itself.

25
00:01:13,245 --> 00:01:14,755
So people simply let it run.

26
00:01:15,045 --> 00:01:17,785
Many wells were never capped, and
water poured across the desert,

27
00:01:17,825 --> 00:01:18,755
day and night.

28
00:01:19,647 --> 00:01:21,157
You can guess how that ended.

29
00:01:21,347 --> 00:01:23,897
The underground water dropped,
and in nineteen sixty-two,

30
00:01:24,127 --> 00:01:27,177
the springs that gave Las
Vegas its name stopped flowing.

31
00:01:27,507 --> 00:01:28,477
The meadows were gone.

32
00:01:31,352 --> 00:01:33,702
Meanwhile, a few miles south of downtown,

33
00:01:33,892 --> 00:01:37,402
along the highway to Los Angeles,
a new kind of business was growing:

34
00:01:37,592 --> 00:01:40,882
big resort casinos, built
just outside the city limits.

35
00:01:41,788 --> 00:01:45,528
In nineteen fifty, the mayor of Las
Vegas wanted to bring them into the city,

36
00:01:45,568 --> 00:01:47,318
so he could tax them.

37
00:01:47,388 --> 00:01:49,698
The casino bosses didn't like that at all.

38
00:01:50,433 --> 00:01:54,093
So they went to the county and asked it
to create a separate town around their

39
00:01:54,133 --> 00:01:54,683
casinos.

40
00:01:55,193 --> 00:01:57,823
Once that town existed,
the city couldn't annex it.

41
00:01:58,113 --> 00:02:00,463
The man leading the
campaign was Gus Greenbaum,

42
00:02:00,593 --> 00:02:04,383
who ran the Flamingo, a casino
opened by the gangster Bugsy Siegel.

43
00:02:05,270 --> 00:02:07,680
The county agreed, in
December nineteen fifty.

44
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:09,880
The new town was called Paradise.

45
00:02:10,270 --> 00:02:12,620
And to this day, most of
the Strip is in Paradise,

46
00:02:12,690 --> 00:02:15,560
run by the county, not
by the city of Las Vegas.

47
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:21,690
Just a month later, Las Vegas
got a very strange new neighbor.

48
00:02:22,261 --> 00:02:26,761
In January nineteen fifty-one, the United
States started testing nuclear bombs at a

49
00:02:26,801 --> 00:02:30,071
site in the desert, about
sixty-five miles from the city.

50
00:02:30,934 --> 00:02:33,184
Many tests happened just before sunrise.

51
00:02:33,454 --> 00:02:37,384
And on clear mornings, people in Las Vegas
could see the flash light up the sky.

52
00:02:38,174 --> 00:02:40,964
Instead of panicking, Las
Vegas turned it into a show.

53
00:02:41,314 --> 00:02:44,344
Hotels with views to the
north held dawn bomb parties.

54
00:02:44,634 --> 00:02:48,304
Guests drank and danced all night, then
went up to the roof to watch the explosion.

55
00:02:49,014 --> 00:02:50,504
Bars served atomic cocktails.

56
00:02:50,774 --> 00:02:53,744
A showgirl at the Sands was
even crowned Miss Atomic Bomb.

57
00:02:54,487 --> 00:02:56,397
But the clouds didn't stay in the desert.

58
00:02:56,707 --> 00:03:00,177
The fallout drifted east,
onto towns in Nevada and Utah,

59
00:03:00,287 --> 00:03:02,717
and many people living
downwind later got sick.

60
00:03:03,087 --> 00:03:06,237
The tests above ground finally
ended in nineteen sixty-two.

61
00:03:09,324 --> 00:03:11,514
So where does Las Vegas
get its water today?

62
00:03:11,704 --> 00:03:12,614
Not from the springs.

63
00:03:13,124 --> 00:03:15,274
About ninety percent of
it comes from one place:

64
00:03:15,424 --> 00:03:17,944
Lake Mead, the giant reservoir
behind the Hoover Dam,

65
00:03:17,984 --> 00:03:19,774
filled by the Colorado River.

66
00:03:20,817 --> 00:03:23,107
And Las Vegas got the
smallest share of all.

67
00:03:23,337 --> 00:03:26,547
In nineteen twenty-eight, when the river's
water was divided between the states,

68
00:03:26,677 --> 00:03:29,487
Las Vegas was a railroad town
of a few thousand people.

69
00:03:29,737 --> 00:03:32,607
So Nevada got just three hundred
thousand acre-feet a year.

70
00:03:32,837 --> 00:03:34,487
That's less than two percent of the river.

71
00:03:34,917 --> 00:03:37,047
California got almost fifteen times more.

72
00:03:37,348 --> 00:03:38,798
And here's the clever part.

73
00:03:39,108 --> 00:03:42,448
Almost all the water that goes
down a drain indoors in Las Vegas,

74
00:03:42,488 --> 00:03:45,408
in showers, sinks, toilets
and hotel kitchens,

75
00:03:45,448 --> 00:03:48,038
is cleaned and sent back into Lake Mead.

76
00:03:48,979 --> 00:03:52,839
For every gallon it returns, the city
is allowed to take another gallon out.

77
00:03:52,879 --> 00:03:56,769
So the water used indoors is
used, cleaned and taken out again.

78
00:03:57,119 --> 00:04:00,239
That's why the hotels, with about
a hundred and fifty thousand rooms,

79
00:04:00,279 --> 00:04:01,569
aren't really the problem.

80
00:04:02,472 --> 00:04:04,222
The real problem is outside.

81
00:04:04,432 --> 00:04:06,462
Water sprayed on grass doesn't come back.

82
00:04:06,552 --> 00:04:09,642
It soaks into the ground, or
evaporates into the desert air.

83
00:04:10,442 --> 00:04:13,012
So Las Vegas declared war on lawns.

84
00:04:13,082 --> 00:04:17,022
The water authority pays people to tear
out their grass and replace it with desert

85
00:04:17,062 --> 00:04:17,712
plants.

86
00:04:18,122 --> 00:04:20,442
And Nevada passed a law:
from twenty twenty-seven,

87
00:04:20,482 --> 00:04:22,902
decorative grass, the
kind nobody walks on,

88
00:04:22,942 --> 00:04:25,232
can't be watered with
Colorado River water.

89
00:04:25,656 --> 00:04:26,926
And it doesn't stop there.

90
00:04:27,156 --> 00:04:30,746
New swimming pools at homes can't be
bigger than six hundred square feet.

91
00:04:30,976 --> 00:04:33,296
And the water authority has
water waste investigators,

92
00:04:33,336 --> 00:04:37,286
who look for sprinklers spraying onto
sidewalks and water running down the street.

93
00:04:37,616 --> 00:04:40,626
Ignore their warning, and you get
a fine that doubles every time.

94
00:04:40,991 --> 00:04:41,371
It works.

95
00:04:41,411 --> 00:04:46,061
Since two thousand and two, the area has
gained almost nine hundred thousand people.

96
00:04:46,271 --> 00:04:49,821
And yet, each resident now uses
fifty-eight percent less water.

97
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:52,900
And the famous fountains of the Bellagio?

98
00:04:52,970 --> 00:04:54,060
They don't use city water.

99
00:04:54,310 --> 00:04:56,690
Their lake is filled mainly
from an old private well,

100
00:04:56,730 --> 00:04:59,460
that once watered a golf
course on the same spot.

101
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:06,290
But even recycled water can't save a
city if the lake itself disappears.

102
00:05:06,939 --> 00:05:10,769
Since the year two thousand, after
years of drought on the Colorado River,

103
00:05:10,999 --> 00:05:14,809
the surface of Lake Mead has dropped
by more than a hundred and fifty feet.

104
00:05:15,319 --> 00:05:18,169
White rings on the rocks show
where the water used to be.

105
00:05:18,986 --> 00:05:22,316
Las Vegas pulls its water out of
the lake through intake pipes,

106
00:05:22,386 --> 00:05:23,606
nicknamed straws.

107
00:05:23,646 --> 00:05:26,556
And as the lake fell, the
city realized that one day,

108
00:05:26,606 --> 00:05:28,436
its straws could be left sucking air.

109
00:05:29,277 --> 00:05:31,647
So it built a third one,
deeper than the others.

110
00:05:32,217 --> 00:05:35,707
Workers dug a tunnel under the lake bed
and connected it to the bottom of the lake.

111
00:05:35,957 --> 00:05:39,637
Then they built a pumping station that
can reach water even if the lake drops to

112
00:05:39,677 --> 00:05:43,707
"dead pool," the level where the Hoover
Dam can no longer release water downstream.

113
00:05:44,606 --> 00:05:46,566
It cost more than a billion dollars.

114
00:05:46,606 --> 00:05:50,726
In twenty twenty-two, when one of the old
intakes stopped working because the water

115
00:05:50,766 --> 00:05:53,176
was too low, the new
pumps were switched on.

116
00:05:54,007 --> 00:05:56,457
As the water fell, the
lake gave up its secrets.

117
00:05:57,007 --> 00:06:00,967
The ruins of Saint Thomas, a small town
flooded in the nineteen thirties when the

118
00:06:01,007 --> 00:06:02,987
lake was filled, came
back out of the water,

119
00:06:03,027 --> 00:06:05,297
and people could walk
through its streets again.

120
00:06:06,172 --> 00:06:07,722
And here's one more surprise.

121
00:06:08,152 --> 00:06:10,872
The Hoover Dam is just
thirty miles from Las Vegas,

122
00:06:10,912 --> 00:06:13,362
but most of its electricity
doesn't go to Nevada.

123
00:06:13,712 --> 00:06:15,542
More than half of it goes to California.

124
00:06:16,192 --> 00:06:17,642
Nevada gets less than a quarter.

125
00:06:18,615 --> 00:06:22,115
The men who built that dam in the nineteen
thirties weren't trusted anywhere near

126
00:06:22,155 --> 00:06:23,025
Las Vegas.

127
00:06:23,475 --> 00:06:25,705
The government built a
whole new town for them,

128
00:06:25,995 --> 00:06:29,555
Boulder City, and banned gambling there,
so the workers wouldn't lose their pay and

129
00:06:29,595 --> 00:06:31,045
the dam would stay on schedule.

130
00:06:31,524 --> 00:06:33,054
The ban is still in place.

131
00:06:33,584 --> 00:06:37,494
Boulder City is one of only two places
in Nevada where gambling is illegal.

132
00:06:37,764 --> 00:06:41,864
And just outside its border, a casino
opened in nineteen thirty-one to catch the

133
00:06:41,904 --> 00:06:43,294
workers on their way home.

134
00:06:43,524 --> 00:06:44,694
It's still open today.

135
00:06:45,576 --> 00:06:48,266
And now the fight over the
river has reached the courts.

136
00:06:48,676 --> 00:06:53,016
In twenty twenty-six, a new federal plan
for the shrinking Colorado River could cut

137
00:06:53,056 --> 00:06:56,666
Nevada's small share by more than
two-thirds in the worst case.

138
00:06:57,116 --> 00:06:58,746
Nevada sued the federal government.

139
00:07:01,132 --> 00:07:03,842
There's one more danger in the
desert, and it's not drought.

140
00:07:03,932 --> 00:07:04,702
It's the opposite.

141
00:07:05,329 --> 00:07:09,589
When a summer storm hits Las Vegas, the
ground is so hard and dry that the rain

142
00:07:09,629 --> 00:07:10,719
doesn't soak in.

143
00:07:10,909 --> 00:07:12,899
In minutes, streets can turn into rivers.

144
00:07:13,830 --> 00:07:18,050
So underneath the city, engineers built
a huge network of flood channels and

145
00:07:18,090 --> 00:07:20,330
tunnels, about six hundred miles of them,

146
00:07:20,370 --> 00:07:22,680
to carry storm water away to Lake Mead.

147
00:07:23,573 --> 00:07:26,743
Most of the time, those
tunnels are dry, dark and cool.

148
00:07:26,933 --> 00:07:28,643
And that's why people live in them.

149
00:07:29,033 --> 00:07:32,773
Outreach workers estimate that between
twelve hundred and fifteen hundred homeless

150
00:07:32,813 --> 00:07:35,723
people may be living in the
tunnels and washes under the city,

151
00:07:35,853 --> 00:07:37,363
some of them right under the Strip.

152
00:07:37,781 --> 00:07:41,191
They build rooms down there, with
beds, furniture and even books.

153
00:07:41,361 --> 00:07:44,731
But when a storm comes, the
tunnels fill with water very fast.

154
00:07:44,961 --> 00:07:46,431
People have died in these floods.

155
00:07:49,350 --> 00:07:51,920
So, how does Las Vegas exist
in the middle of a desert?

156
00:07:52,537 --> 00:07:55,267
It drained its own springs,
and moved on to a river.

157
00:07:55,517 --> 00:07:59,627
It drinks most of its water twice, and
pays people to tear out their lawns.

158
00:07:59,717 --> 00:08:03,517
It dug a straw under a shrinking lake,
and built six hundred miles of tunnels for

159
00:08:03,557 --> 00:08:05,147
rain that almost never comes.

160
00:08:06,092 --> 00:08:08,712
And the Strip, the thing
everyone thinks of as Las Vegas,

161
00:08:08,752 --> 00:08:10,382
isn't even in Las Vegas.

162
00:08:10,993 --> 00:08:12,983
So, would you live in
a city in the desert?

163
00:08:13,133 --> 00:08:14,963
And which city should we explain next?

164
00:08:15,093 --> 00:08:16,083
Tell me in the comments.

165
00:08:16,393 --> 00:08:17,483
This was How It's Run.
